  "worked_solution": "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n\u2200x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x>0$\" is ${Z}^{+},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$x\\le 0$\" is ${Z}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={Z}^{+}\\cup {Z}_{0}^{-}=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $\u2200x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
